#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561081} :activity/id #uuid "db102532-087a-5b88-8ea6-b910df7d8715" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048142 :topic/id #uuid "82d65d50-f769-531a-8e21-07640e0f163f" :topic/math-academy-id 2215} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039ad15f-aa14-5833-b492-297d15ec8481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560368} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb8b6d5-6d81-50d2-85a0-0755a8afe296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560593} {:answer/id #uuid "897b3115-f689-53ea-8b8f-579dc209df81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-2\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560648} {:answer/id #uuid "89d2e657-a6a8-59f6-8532-79d2b8c65bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=-\\frac{50}{9}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560650} {:answer/id #uuid "af390c1b-5127-52ed-92c3-72c04d0ab64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=2\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560650} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee0f6b8-65be-5837-90b0-536ef0f796f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2e69b893-dca1-5a00-a0a1-d673b040380f" :question/math-academy-id "q-44983" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{3{a}_{n}}{5},\\,\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{10}{3},\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{10}{3}.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=r\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=\\frac{10}{3}$ and $r=-\\frac{3}{5}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{-1}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10\\cdot 5}{3\\cdot 3}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} \\\\  & =-\\frac{50}{9}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{50}{9}\\cdot {(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8636cc73-2e13-568c-ad29-370477db4596" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "227897ea-9dd1-5b96-9c06-008a8a2d99f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n+1)=6f(n),\\,f(1)=12,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560443} {:answer/id #uuid "2b933277-b836-55ae-9b21-a1a99505fdc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n+1)=2f(n),\\,f(1)=12,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560458} {:answer/id #uuid "3a562547-fbeb-5f3b-853f-ddbd84961e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n+1)=6f(n),\\,f(1)=2,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560495} {:answer/id #uuid "71f17afc-b6a1-50e6-8314-cf085641ebf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n+1)=2f(n),\\,f(1)=6,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560606} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6698c3-0e55-55e1-a6c5-bc756af04932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n+1)=2f(n),\\,f(1)=3,\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560458} :answer-field/id #uuid "66bae46d-9a2a-5dcb-a638-46a59f8b52be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "715d8ed0-87e8-5b42-a40f-879649ce1b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-44995" :question/problem "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is $f(n)=3\\cdot {2}^{n+1}$ for $n\\ge 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n).\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we must determine the common ratio $r$ and state the first term ${a}_{1}.$ The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =3\\cdot {2}^{1+1}=3\\cdot 4=12, \\\\ f(2) & =3\\cdot {2}^{2+1}=3\\cdot 8=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)}=\\frac{24}{12}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula for the geometric  sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=2f(n),\\,f(1)=12,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b4bbf74d-c726-5214-968d-de225c1af191"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca374ed-64c4-537e-9df7-38e602f55fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560463} {:answer/id #uuid "4301ea6f-1c49-5f63-a937-a2c2069919c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n)={2}^{n},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560514} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e66bc-7a14-5aba-9a6a-a17f030fcf54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n)={2}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560527} {:answer/id #uuid "679a841f-b122-5f85-a654-2b11ecc33553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{1}{{4}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560581} {:answer/id #uuid "955d3208-98c9-5cd1-a334-320a252d84ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}},\\,n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560463} :answer-field/id #uuid "9947e7a8-c30a-59fb-977d-989052168899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "072376b1-6413-51af-98f6-95587e3acbf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-44986" :question/problem "What is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of the geometric sequence given by\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{2},\\,\\,f(1)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,n\\ge 1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n+1)=\\frac{f(n)}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}f(n)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\nf(n)=f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nSo, we must determine the first term $f(1)$ and the common ratio $r.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is $f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=r\\cdot f(n)\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $r=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the formula for the $n$th term, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =f(1)\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by manipulating the exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(n) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\nf(n)=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}},\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c5c7edc3-8b0c-53c9-8c90-bfaf896e93bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561088}}] :activity/title "Translating Between Explicit and Recursive Formulas for Geometric Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560358}]}
